UID:0u6rb60dflsqud69925g60iqtf@google.com
CLASS:PUBLIC
CREATED:20261014T214933Z
DESCRIPTION:<b>AOS</b>: 10:03:27 UTC<br>RX: <b>145.800 MHz</b> - FM over Eu
 rope<br>Language: Frensh<br><br><b>School Information:<br></b>The "Ecole In
 ternationale PACA" is located in Manosque\, South-East of France at 20 km f
 rom the giant ITER construction site (International Thermonuclear Experimen
 tal Reactor). <br>The project of contacting the ISS arose from two goals. F
 irst\, given the current international context\, it became desirable to sho
 wcase symbols of unity. The international school EIPACA\, the ITER consorti
 um and the ISS all seemed good examples of a collaboration for the greater 
 good. The second goal was to promote sciences and technology among students
 \, most importantly women. The international school EIPACA occupies a singu
 lar position within the French education system. The numbers speak for them
 selves: 900 students\, 47 nationalities\, 6 language sections and 1 europea
 n school. Designed from the beginning to welcome the children of the ITER s
 taff\, the international consortium on nuclear fusion gathering South Korea
 \, Japan\, the EU\, Switzerland\, the USA\, China\, India and Russia\, the 
 International School is by design a place where multicultural identities de
 velop and blossom. <br>The project of a radio contact between the ISS and t
 he EIPACA aimed at showcasing sciences as a treasured place of collaboratio
 n and communication between cultures\, allowing us to focus on what units u
 s. The radiocontact with the ISS was also an opportunity to put illustrate 
 and contextualize some elements of the different syllabi. Students from 11t
 h and 12th grades studied and performed experiments on the signal modulatio
 n and demodulation for AM and FM. Middle school students used the ISS and t
 he radiocontact as an opportunity to learn about circular motions\, orbits\
 , gravity and electromagnetic waves. <br>Even at the elementary school leve
 l\, pupils were introduced to spatial observation and sciences. Although th
 e challenges were different at every level\, tackling them at the same time
  allowed us to foster a sense of unity among students of different ages and
  backgrounds. We hope this will have helped students finding purpose in the
 ir science classes. Noble as these ideals may be\, it would not have been p
 ossible to implement them in practice if it were not for the help and colla
 boration of the Radioclub from the Centre d'astronomie de St-Michel-L'Obser
 vatoire. Their skills and devotion allowed our students to experience the r
 adio through various workshops\, ranging from radio-detection to radio-comm
 unication. Most importantly though\, it is thanks to their unwavering suppo
 rt that the contact with the ISS is now possible. We are tremendously grate
 ful for their help.<br><b><br>Questions:<br></b>1. Comment votre entraineme
 nt sur Terre vous permet-il de vous adapter a la vie en micro-gravite?<br>2
 . Est-ce que vous devez faire regulierement des ajustements a votre traject
 oire pour rester sur l'orbite prevue? Si oui\, comment determinez-vous quel
 les corrections doivent etre faites?<br>3. Est-ce que voir la Terre depuis 
 l'ISS a un impact sur la maniere dont vous percevez les choses\, et si oui 
 lequel?<br>4. Quelle est votre activite favorite a bord de l'ISS?<br>5. Que
  faites-vous sur votre temps libre?<br>6. La vie a bord de l'ISS est-elle c
 onforme a ce a quoi vous vous attendiez?<br>7. Par quels moyens parvenez-vo
 us a garder le contact avec vos proches sur Terre?<br>8. Avez-vous le meme 
 acces a Internet que celui dont vous jouissiez sur Terre?<br>9. Avez-vous d
 eja fait une sortie extra-vehiculaire? Quel effet cela fait-il?<br>10. Est-
 ce que vous pouvez voir la France depuis l'ISS?<br>11. Est-ce que vous avez
  le vertige dans l'espace? Qu'est-ce que l'on ressent quand on est en chute
  libre?<br>12. Est-ce que vous organisez des activites collectives avec les
  autres astronautes de la station?<br>13. Avez-vous du faire face a des pro
 blemes inattendus au cours de votre mission? Si oui\, lesquels?<br>14. Les 
 astronautes ont ils parfois des nausees ou des troubles dus a l'apesanteur?
  La digestion et la circulation sanguine sont-elles impactees?<br>15. Quell
 e est la situation la plus difficile a laquelle vous avez du vous preparer?
 <br>16. Quel est votre role au sein de l'ISS? Quelles sont les taches les p
 lus interessantes que vous ayez eu a faire?<br>17. Comment gerez-vous le st
 ock de nourriture pour la duree de la mission? Est-ce qu'il faut se rationn
 er?<br>18. A quelle frequence avez-vous des contacts avec la Terre depuis l
 'ISS?<br>19. Comment renouvelez-vous l'air a bord de l'ISS?<br>20. Est-ce q
 ue le fait d'etre dans l'espace vous interroge quant a la place de l'Humain
  dans l'Univers?<br>21. Avez-vous le choix dans la nourriture lors de chaqu
 e repas?
LAST-MODIFIED:20261014T214934Z
LOCATION:Ecole Internationale Manosque\, 159 Av. du Dr Bernard Foussier\, 0
 4100 Manosque\, France
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:OR4ISS (Joshua Kutryk KJ5BFU) - 53 deg -  F4KKM (Manosque\, France)
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
